# How much of its SMT resource limit a verified function may use
# (verus/count --rlimit; verus/README.md, "Headroom"): the rlimit count
# Verus's statistics report for the function, summed over its queries, as
# a share of its `#[verifier::rlimit(N)]` or of the default (10), at
# 3,000,000 a unit. Z3's count is deterministic, so a function near its
# limit passes every run of the change that put it there and fails on main
# once a change beside it, or to a definition it unfolds, adds a little.
#
# `max-share P` holds every function to P percent. `allow NAME P` lets one
# function, named as Verus names it, go above that, to P: a function that
# was there before this check, whose headroom is still to be restored.
# The check also compares this file with the base revision's: max-share
# may not rise, and an allow line may be dropped or lowered but not added
# or raised. A raised `#[verifier::rlimit(N)]` lowers a share as an allow
# line would, so a function whose limit is above the base revision's (the
# default where it had none) needs an `rlimit-raised NAME N REASON` line
# here, new in that change, for that function and N.
#
# Restore headroom before raising a limit: split the function, narrow what
# it unfolds (`opaque`, `reveal` in one scope, a lemma in place of an
# inline proof). Raise the rlimit last, to the smallest N that holds the
# function within max-share, and say in REASON what it uses on its own
# prover (`{FUNCTION_EXAMPLE}: 17.5M, 58% of the default and 19% of 30`).
max-share 65
